---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import NextChapter from '../components/NextChapter.astro';
---

<Base title="Energy efficiency" active="efficiency/">
  <PageHead slug="efficiency/">
    Doing the same things with less energy. In Atlanta homes that starts with LED bulbs, efficient appliances and
    smart thermostats, and goes as far as new insulation and heat pumps.
  </PageHead>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="split split--text-wide">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Many older homes lose energy through poor insulation and outdated systems, and these can be fixed. Adding
          insulation, sealing leaks and installing heat pumps make homes far more efficient. Those upgrades lower
          bills, ease demand on the grid and cut greenhouse gas emissions, in line with Atlanta's sustainability goals.
        </p>
        <h2>The housing envelope</h2>
        <p>
          The envelope is your home's outer shell: walls, roof, windows, floor and foundation. It controls how much
          heat, air and moisture get in or out. A poorly insulated or leaky envelope means higher bills and a less
          comfortable home.
        </p>
        <p>
          Adding insulation, sealing air leaks and improving the attic can cut household energy use by about{' '}
          <strong>20 to 30 percent</strong> in Atlanta homes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure src="ee_house.png" alt="Cutaway of an energy-efficient home showing insulation, sealed windows and a heat pump" variant="bare" max={360} />
    </div>
    <Figure src="ee_energy_savings.png" alt="Chart comparing household energy use before and after envelope improvements" caption="Household energy use before and after envelope upgrades" max={820} />
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Heat pumps</h2>
        <p>
          Heat pumps move heat instead of making it. In winter they pull heat in from the outside air; in summer they
          pump it out of your home.
        </p>
        <p>
          They're 2.5 to 4 times more efficient than gas furnaces or electric resistance heaters, and Atlanta's mild
          winters suit them well year-round. Replacing an older system with a heat pump can cut heating energy by up
          to half.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure src="ee_impact_heat_pump.png" alt="Bar chart of annual energy use for a typical pre-1980 Atlanta home with and without a heat pump" caption="Annual energy use for a typical pre-1980 Atlanta home, with and without a heat pump" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why upgrades matter together</h2>
        <p>
          Older homes waste energy through poor insulation, air leaks and outdated equipment. Upgrading the envelope
          and switching to a heat pump together can nearly halve energy use and significantly reduce carbon
          emissions.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure src="ee_impact_full_retrofit.png" alt="Bar chart comparing annual energy use with envelope upgrades, a heat pump, and both" caption="Annual energy use with envelope and heat pump upgrades" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What you save</h2>
        <p>
          Better insulation, smarter heating and efficient appliances use less electricity and gas, lowering bills by
          hundreds of dollars a year.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both all-electric and gas-heated homes benefit, especially older ones. The same upgrades also make homes
          healthier and more comfortable.
        </p>
        <Figure src="ee_what_if.png" alt="Illustration asking what would change if homes were upgraded" variant="bare" max={340} />
      </div>
      <Figure src="ee_annual_cost_savings.png" alt="Bar chart of annual household energy cost savings by home type" caption="Annual household energy cost savings by home type" />
    </div>
    <p class="sources">Sources: DOE, EIA, Southface, NREL, ENERGY STAR and Georgia Power data estimates.</p>
  </section>

  <NextChapter slug="efficiency/" />
</Base>
